{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J1 at head d87ea2b1, for request 7d6ce0f6 and review request 6aec789d. Verdict: APPROVED. The fence now lifts only on an answer to the request it holds, and the five items of 384a99aa stay repaired. The head is hosted code and may land. Deploying it stays with the planner, whose b328327d opens the endpoint at that deployment.\n\n## Your three questions\n\n1. **The digest comparison: I found no way for a press on the right row to fail.** The send hands `intentDigest(signed.intent)` to `sending.submitting`, and `Acted.digest` is the same function of the same intent. The scope names the row by `intentDigest` of the intent that it parsed from the kept bytes. A signed intent's digest does not cover the unsigned parts, so a row kept again with other unsigned parts still matches. An `unknown` fence is reached only from `submitting`, and `submitting` sets the digest in the same step. The case that stays is the one section 9 states: no row is left to press, and the fence stays until a reload.\n2. **The memory key: I found no second address that matters.** The page's `sessionOf` uses `location.origin`, and an origin is already normal in case and port. A second address of one service, such as a custom domain, is a different origin and so a different page load, which starts with empty memory anyway. Trimming the slash does no harm. The context also holds the membership's scope and incarnation and the key, so a new incarnation or a new key has lost nothing.\n3. **The untraced refusal: it does not belong to this head, and it needs its own request.** `packages/scope/src/signed-reads.ts` is unchanged from main, so J1 did not cause it. Pages with a session do not meet it, and the witness's second device holds one. Your sentence says a new device on signed reads \"may fail to open a room\". That matters on a Worker where sessions are off or refused, because `openRoom` reads the directory first. I did not trace it either. File it as an item with the steps that produced it, and do not fix it here.\n\n## Your other points\n\n- **The fence.** `settledAssociations` admits only `landed` and `refused`. Line 680's assertion and the four named controls hold that.\n- **The older row.** `liftFences` compares the digest on both fences. An older row lifts neither.\n- **The 18 controls not rerun: accepted, and I do not need the four minutes.** I confirmed that from `48511905` to `5ad1b308`, `packages/scope` changes only the comment in `originals.ts`, and that `48511905` already includes main. So the broken lines and the code around them are the same.\n- **The provider's figures.** The comment and the note state them as Cloudflare's, and they state what nobody checked.\n\n## The gate that failed first\n\n`finding.scope.test.ts:107` failed once at this commit, and the second gate passed with no source change. A passing rerun does not show that the test is sound, so I checked whether J1 can reach it. J1 adds `originals.wake()` to the object's wake and an `expire` call to its alarm. With no row, `wake()` answers null and adds no time, and that test keeps no copy. The test's own path is `cancel-merge` and `r.settle()`. So I accept the second gate for this head, and I leave the cause with c5f0cff9. This is a judgment from reading the code. Nobody has named the cause, and nothing has run to rule J1 out.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. The key that replaces the `WeakSet` states what the memory means, and it removes the test's need for a cloned session. `original-check.ts` now imports a type from `shell.ts`, which adds no runtime dependency.\n\n**Security.** No authority issue. Holding the fence for `gone`, `mismatch` and `archived` errs toward no second send. A session still reaches only its member's rows, and nothing here changes what a session can make.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. `liftFences` keeps the rule in one tested place. An unbounded `Set` of held contexts lives only for one page load, and it grows by one for each room and key.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The whole source diff from f63f0015, the design note's and implementation note's changes, and the evidence below. I read the test diff only where it names the cases above.\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `4871fd25…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `5ad1b308`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3242 plain and 928 native tests. The witness and controls record the same commit. All 36 and 9 control rows distinguish, and the witness reports no failures. The diff from there to this head touches only the note and its manifest. I read those results and did not rerun them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `original-copy.scope.test.ts` and `retained-editor.scope.test.ts` pass 23 of 23. `shell.test.ts`, `view.test.ts` and `join.test.ts` pass 20 of 20. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 with 0 failures. `check-kernel-boundary` reports 127 sources and 0 refusals. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean. The head includes main `032e4f40`.\n- **Not done.** I reproduced neither the `gone` path nor the older-row path in a browser, and I used no independent reader on this repair. An independent reader read the first build, not this one. No Worker, real room or second physical device has run this code, and the first deployment is where the rest of J1's limits meet real use.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3a09e229161bc187eee56402b4360c384a41d6ed","binding":"assigned","head":"d87ea2b1010b6436400e5ff6db94f9b9d6b6ca3a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e70f36d85d290847f80b3f2892bbeb0af4a17c6a\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3649cf4ac25e995f657730259cbc8b1b415812d9\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f757